		{
			"id": "eAhzkgNzbb4S-gf29",
			"description": "Missile Launcher",
			"local_notes": "Shoulder-fired, one missile loaded at a time; the gunner (or a loader) carries spares separately (see the missile entries below). Flakk missiles trade blast for a proximity fuze and are wasted against ground targets.\n\nLore: The Guard's man-portable answer to armour and aircraft when a lascannon isn't to hand: fiction repeatedly has missile teams crack light and medium vehicles and occasionally cripple a Leman Russ, while frag loads are the squad's answer to entrenched infantry and light structures.\n\nDesign: No single framework anchor covers this launcher as a whole, but Krak is itself one of the project's fixed anchors: 6dx6(3) cr, a one-shot, disposable shaped charge. Checked with the exact dice distribution (avg. 126) against the AV table: it beats AV10 (DR 100, eff. DR 33) essentially always and AV11-12 (DR 150/200, eff. DR 50/66) 100%/99% of the time - reliably wrecks Chimeras and Rhinos; against AV13 (DR 275, eff. DR 91) it's still 90% - reliable vs AV12-13 as the anchor requires; and against AV14 (DR 350, eff. DR 116) it drops to 64% - \"about two in three\" - so a Land Raider or Leman Russ's frontal glacis is a real but not certain kill, which is why the fiction has missile teams engage such targets in numbers rather than solo. Frag: scaled from the B414 fragmentation rules as a \"big frag grenade\" per the framework's own description - double the concussion dice and 1.5x the fragmentation dice of a standard hand grenade (3d cr ex [6d cut]) to reflect a much larger warhead, giving 6d cr ex [9d cut]; it has no armor divisor and is not intended to threaten anything on the vehicle AV table, only infantry and light cover. Flakk: the frag warhead's dice with a proximity fuze instead of impact/frag optimisation, at one die less on each figure (5d cr ex [8d cut]) since more of the casing goes into the fuze and less into the frag liner; it is not rated against ground DR at all. Acc/range/RoF/Bulk are shared across usages since they describe the launcher, not the warhead, and are built as a shoulder-fired tube (lower ST and Bulk penalty than the tripod weapons above, since one Guardsman carries and fires it solo).",
